To check if a number is a power of three without using loops or recursion, we can use the bit manipulation technique. The idea is to keep shifting the bits of the number by one position to the right and check if the number is divisible by three after each shift. If at any point the number becomes zero or becomes an odd number and is not divisible by three, then it is not a power of three. Here is the Java code for the same:

```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return false; // base case
    int power = 1;
    while (n > 1) {
        if (n % 3 != 0 || (power * 3 > Integer.MAX_VALUE / 3 && n != 2147483647)) {
            return false;
        }
        n = n / 3;
        power *= 3;
    }
    return true;
}
```

Explanation:

1. We first check if the number is less than or equal to zero and return false if it is.
2. We initialize a variable `power` to 1 and start shifting the bits of the number `n` to the right.
3. After each shift, we check if the number is divisible by three or not. If it is not divisible by three or if the value of `power` is going to exceed the maximum value that can be represented by an integer, then we return false.
4. If we have shifted all the bits of the number and we are left with only one bit (i.e., `n` is now 1), then we have found that `n` is a power of three and return true.

The time complexity of this solution is O(log3(n)) as we are performing log3(n) shifts on the number.

